Here’s a small, self‑contained React component that implements a classic todo list with **add**, **complete**, and **delete** functionality. It uses React hooks (`useState`) and plain CSS for basic styling, but you can adapt the styles to fit your project.

```jsx
import React, { useState } from "react";
import "./TodoList.css";   // optional – see CSS snippet below

// ---------------------------
// TodoList Component
// ---------------------------
export default function TodoList() {
  // Each todo is an object: { id, text, completed }
  const [todos, setTodos] = useState([]);
  const [newTodo, setNewTodo] = useState("");

  // Add a new todo
  const handleAdd = (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    const trimmed = newTodo.trim();
    if (!trimmed) return;

    const todo = {
      id: Date.now(),               // simple unique id
      text: trimmed,
      completed: false,
    };
    setTodos([todo, ...todos]);    // newest on top
    setNewTodo("");
  };

  // Toggle completed state
  const toggleComplete = (id) => {
    setTodos(
      todos.map((t) =>
        t.id === id ? { ...t, completed: !t.completed } : t
      )
    );
  };

  // Delete a todo
  const handleDelete = (id) => {
    setTodos(todos.filter((t) => t.id !== id));
  };

  return (
    <div className="todo-container">
      <h2>Todo List</h2>

      {/* New‑todo form */}
      <form onSubmit={handleAdd} className="todo-form">
        <input
          type="text"
          placeholder="What needs to be done?"
          value={newTodo}
          onChange={(e) => setNewTodo(e.target.value)}
        />
        <button type="submit">Add</button>
      </form>

      {/* List of todos */}
      <ul className="todo-list">
        {todos.map((todo) => (
          <li
            key={todo.id}
            className={todo.completed ? "completed" : ""}
          >
            <span
              onClick={() => toggleComplete(todo.id)}
              className="todo-text"
            >
              {todo.text}
            </span>

            <button
              onClick={() => handleDelete(todo.id)}
              className="delete-btn"
            >
              ✕
            </button>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      {/* Empty state */}
      {todos.length === 0 && (
        <p className="empty-msg">Your list is empty. Add a task above!</p>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Optional CSS (`TodoList.css`)

```css
.todo-container {
  max-width: 400px;
  margin: 2rem auto;
  padding: 1rem;
  border: 1px solid #ddd;
  border-radius: 6px;
  font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
  background: #fafafa;
}

.todo-form {
  display: flex;
  gap: 0.5rem;
  margin-bottom: 1rem;
}

.todo-form input {
  flex: 1;
  padding: 0.5rem;
  border: 1px solid #bbb;
  border-radius: 4px;
}

.todo-form button {
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
  border: none;
  background: #28a745;
  color: #fff;
  border-radius: 4px;
  cursor: pointer;
}

.todo-form button:hover {
  background: #218838;
}

.todo-list {
  list-style: none;
  padding: 0;
  margin: 0;
}

.todo-list li {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  padding: 0.5rem;
  border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;
}

.todo-list li.completed .todo-text {
  text-decoration: line-through;
  color: #777;
}

.todo-text {
  flex: 1;
  cursor: pointer;
}

.delete-btn {
  background: transparent;
  border: none;
  color: #dc3545;
  font-size: 1.2rem;
  cursor: pointer;
  padding: 0 0.2rem;
}

.delete-btn:hover {
  color: #c82333;
}

.empty-msg {
  text-align: center;
  color: #555;
  margin-top: 1rem;
}
```

### How it works
1. **State** – `todos` holds an array of todo objects, each with `id`, `text`, and `completed`. `newTodo` holds the current input value.
2. **Add** – Submitting the form creates a new todo (using the current timestamp as a simple unique id) and prepends it to the list.
3. **Complete** – Clicking the todo text toggles its `completed` flag, which adds a CSS class to strike‑through the text.
4. **Delete** – The “✕” button removes the item from the array.

You can drop the component (and optional CSS) into any React project, import it, and start using it right away. Feel free to expand it with persistence (e.g., `localStorage`), filtering, or drag‑and‑drop reordering as needed. Enjoy building!
